after.text:
// every id a format 3 review has
interface Step     { id: number }          // from 1; no other step has it
interface Note     { id: number }          // from 1; no other note has it
interface Contract { id: number | string } // a number from 1 that no other contract has, linked #contract:3;
                                           // a name still reads, with a warning to number it

introduction.text:
Before this change, a format 3 review numbered steps and notes from one counter, and prose linked them as a bare `#7`. Those reviews still read: a bare link goes to whichever kind has the number, and `--check` asks to name the kind. The fallback holds only while no step and no note share a number, and the new per-kind counter is what makes them share one:

{{diagram:bare}}

The steps follow a bare link to where it lands, then show how a new id makes it land nowhere.

A link goes from text in a message to a table (#contract:2) and then to a place on the screen:

{{diagram:path}}

`link.find` reads the kind, `follow_three` looks only in that kind, and `go.follow` opens a walk, a note or a contract page. `link.to` goes the other way and writes the link for a conversation (#contract:4).

prose.text:
For #note:2, in the review clone's working tree only (`/tmp/review-c`, not committed): the loop now also checks `why`, each block's `text`, each promise's `text` and each use's `note`, each with its own place in the message:

{{chunk:check-contracts-now}}

With the same contract, the script now reports all four (`contract 1, why`, `contract 1`, `contract 1, block 1`, `contract 1, promise 1`). `SPEC=three make test` and `SPEC=prose make test` pass (18 and 19 tests).

The change does not touch `skills/answering-threads/SKILL.md`, which is how agents learn the format (#contract:1). It still says:

{{chunk:skill-links}}

and, about what `show.lua` prints, which now reads `#step:1` and `#contract:1`:

{{chunk:skill-known-by}}

So an agent following the skill writes named contracts and bare links, and every one of them gets a `--check` warning. If it adds steps to an old review, it walks into #note:1. The skill's example review (`[[furthest]]`, `#2`, `#3`) needs the same update.
